use /lib
use /ui
from /scope/prompt-mode/modes import PromptModesScope
from /scope/user-preferences import UserPreferencesScope
from /ui/components/inset-panel import InsetPanelScope
from /ui/components/scrollbar import ScrollbarScope
from /ui/theme import ThemeScope

export ui-component ThemeEditorScope:
    concept: {ThemeEditorConcept}

concept ThemeEditorConcept:
    pitch:
        Where the @{ThemeScope}'s base colour, and the colours of Code, Chain,
        Spec, and Ask, of the @{PromptModesScope}, are chosen in place of the
        theme's own, or put back to them.

    shape: {ThemeEditorShape}

shape ThemeEditorShape:
    description:
        Shown in an @{InsetPanelScope} over the main window, in place of
        anything else there, like the settings. It is headed "Theme", with a
        close button whose tooltip says it closes the theme editor, and a line
        beneath the heading; closing the panel any way an inset panel closes
        closes it too. Opening it while it is already open leaves it as it
        is. Beneath the heading, its page scrolls when it is taller than the
        panel.

    page:
        The page is headed "Colours", with the mode buttons at its right,
        and beneath, a muted line saying that dark and light mode each keep
        their own colours, that these are the mode showing's, and that a
        colour shows everywhere as soon as it is chosen and is kept for the
        next launch. Then a row for each colour, in order: Base, Code, Chain,
        Spec, and Ask. Last, a "Reset all" button.

    modes:
        Two small ghost buttons, "Dark mode" with a moon icon and "Light mode"
        with a sun icon, the one showing selected. The colours shown and
        edited are always the mode showing's. Clicking the other shows that
        mode, as the ribbon's brightness would, and the rows then show its
        colours. Each tooltip says it shows that mode and edits its colours.

    rows:
        Each row has gpui-kit's colour picker, showing the colour, then the
        colour's name, its value as a hex code such as #444444, muted, and,
        while it hasn't been chosen, "Theme's own", muted; beneath them, a
        muted line saying what it colours. At the right end, a small outline
        "Reset" button puts back the theme's own colour, disabled while it is
        already the theme's own, its tooltip saying which.

        What each colours:

        - Base: the window and its panels. Every other grey keeps its step from it, and brightness moves them all from here. It shows the base at brightness 0.
        - Code: Code's tab, and whatever shows Code or was sent in it.
        - Chain: the chain's tab, Code and Spec together. Unless chosen, it is midway between Code and Spec.
        - Spec: Spec's tab, and whatever shows Spec or was sent in it.
        - Ask: Ask's tab, and the questions asked in it.

        Picking a colour chooses it for the mode showing: it shows
        everywhere at once, the row showing its new hex code, and is saved
        among the @{UserPreferencesScope}. A colour that can't be saved
        still holds for the session, and fails quietly.

    resetAll:
        "Reset all" puts every colour of the mode showing back to the
        theme's own, leaving the other mode's as they are. It is disabled
        while the mode showing has none chosen, its tooltip saying so.

    following:
        The pickers follow the mode showing and its colours however they are
        changed, such as by the ribbon's Brightness slider crossing into the
        other mode, or a Reset.
